Connor Reed from North Wales was working  in a school in Wuhan when he began to  feel unwell he would become the first  known British man to catch the corona  virus take me back to November 25th day  one you get a cold the end of November I  was feeling and just a normal cold and  the problem with this virus is it  progresses in stages so you'll have a  cold you'll get better and you'll get  worse you'll get better then you get  worse again it's quite possible Connor  just happen to catch his cold several  days before he was then struck by the  corona virus just described the symptoms  and where it really starts to hit I was  feeling achy I just wanted to curl up  into a ball I had ear problems and and  no sinus problems where it was it felt  like there was a balloon being blown up  and in my face I was raking coughing it  was terrible and it was happening so  much and lost my voice and and I've lost  my voice a few times just just in a  little bit but this was it was gone I  couldn't make any sound at all for most  people who catch the virus it won't get  any worse than this and may not even get  as bad but for Connor the scariest part  was when he began to struggle with his  breathing it was a case of going to bed  and waking up not being able to breathe  and me it scared me because breathing's  necessities a life like if you have the  flu you really you feel like you're  going to die be you're really not but  when your lungs getting affected that's  where it scared me and I couldn't take a  full breath I know the breaths I did  take it sounded like I was breathing  through a back  connor was diagnosed with pneumonia  despite living in what would become the  epicenter of the virus as yet the local  authorities were unaware that Wuhan in  China was incubating a nasty new virus  that would spread across the globe  it was weeks before Connor was told he  had Co vid 19  he's now recovered from the illness but  his adopted City is still struggling  with strict rules on how often you can  leave the house at the moment  one person is allowed to leave their  house every three days and that's just a  purchased necessities there are some  shops that are open they've blocked the  door off and you tell them what you want  they put it into a bag and then they  past the back - you want a stick and  just to avoid that personal contact  Connor says he always thought he would  pull through but having experienced it  he can see why the virus could be deadly  for some Jason Ferrell Sky News
 

